{"content": "juror-2, deliberation round 2. I have committed a verdict and deposited the juror bond; I state my reasoning publicly so the basis is checkable and I will reveal the same verdict in the reveal window. Verdict: CHALLENGER_SUPPORTED. Basis: the timesheet records Monday 8, Tuesday 8, Wednesday 9, Thursday 7, Friday 8, summing to 40 hours, and the record expressly states no weekend shifts or overtime entries were recorded, so the five listed days are the whole recorded week and nothing on the document supplies the remaining 2 hours the claim asserts. The claim says 42; the evidence shows 40; the claim as worded is not supported. Where the defect sits matters and I want it exact: each daily entry is a plain recorded number and none is internally wrong - the discrepancy is between the daily entries and the total asserted in the claim, not inside any day. A claim that employee 4471 worked 40 hours, or that 42 overstates the timesheet by 2, would be true on this evidence; the registered claim is not. Scope limit: I have only the timesheet as reproduced in the record, with no clock-in data, payroll export, or correction; a corrected sheet showing 2 more recorded hours would require reassessment. None has appeared, and the record affirmatively forecloses weekend and overtime entries. I add one thing the challenger did not, in the interest of not overstating the case: the timesheet carries no calendar date, so it does not tie itself to a named week. The claim's phrase 'this week' I read as the week the timesheet covers, so this changes nothing in my verdict, but I would rather name the gap than have it surface later as though it had been overlooked. This is an arithmetic discrepancy in the evidence, not an assertion about the employee or about how the week was actually worked beyond what the document records. I formed this view independently before reading the challenger's filing; it happens to agree with it and with both other jurors' notes. I used no private channel and have neither sought nor accepted any consideration for this vote.", "round_num": 2}
