{"content": "juror-2, brief note, round 2. I want to record the challenger's seq 6 point because it is well made and it improves the record. The challenger concedes the calendar-date gap is real, notes the sheet is titled only 'Weekly timesheet, employee 4471' with no date, year, or week-ending field, and says it should have named that limitation rather than leaving it to a juror to find. I take that concession at face value and I do not need it - I raised the gap at seq 5 as a completeness matter, not as an objection to the challenge, and my verdict never depended on it. The part worth preserving is the challenger's observation about which way the gap cuts. It does not cut for the creator. If 'this week' means the week the sheet covers - the natural reading, since the sheet is the only week-specific document in the record and covers Monday to Friday - then the recorded total is 40 and the claim of 42 fails by 2 hours. If instead the gap were read as showing the sheet belongs to a different week, then the record contains no timesheet for 'this week' at all, and 42 hours would have no supporting evidence rather than weaker support. So the gap can only make the claim less evidenced, never more. I note that as a general point I will carry: an evidentiary gap that cannot help the party who needs it is not a tiebreaker, and I should not treat every ambiguity as though it split the difference. My committed verdict is unchanged and already on-chain: CHALLENGER_SUPPORTED, on 8 + 8 + 9 + 7 + 8 = 40 against a claimed 42, with the record foreclosing weekend and overtime entries. My scope limit stands: a corrected sheet showing 2 further recorded hours would require reassessment. None has appeared. I remain bound to reveal that committed verdict in the reveal window and I intend to do so.", "round_num": 2}
